<div class="migration-page"><header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a> / Website purpose</nav><h1>Define Your Website’s Purpose and Target Audience</h1><p class="intro">Before choosing a layout, decide what your website needs to accomplish and who it needs to help. Those two decisions shape the content, features and next steps your visitors will see.</p></header><article><section><h2>1. Choose the business outcome</h2><p>A business website can sell products, generate inquiries, educate buyers, support customers or introduce a brand. It can serve several purposes, but each important page should make its primary job clear.</p><p>For a service business, that job may be helping the right customer request an estimate. For an online store, it may be helping a shopper choose and purchase a product. A support page may need to answer an existing customer’s question without requiring a call.</p><p>Write the outcome in a sentence: “This page helps [audience] understand [need] and take [action].” Choose a secondary action for visitors who are interested but not ready.</p></section>
<section><h2>2. Research the people you want to reach</h2><p>Start with what customers ask before they buy. Review genuine inquiries, sales conversations and common objections. Ask which services they need, where they are located, what they already know and what would prevent them from choosing a provider.</p><p>Demographics can provide context, but the practical need and decision stage often explain more. Someone requesting emergency repair needs a different path from someone researching a future renovation.</p><p>Record motivations and buying behavior without inventing a detailed persona from assumptions. Use customer evidence to refine the picture over time.</p></section>
<section><h2>3. Explain why your business is a fit</h2><p>Your value proposition connects the customer’s need with a credible reason to choose you. Explain the work you do, who it is for, where it is available and what makes the experience useful.</p><p>Support that explanation with facts: relevant project examples, genuine reviews, clear service scope or qualifications you actually hold. Avoid “best” claims and generic promises that any competitor could make.</p><p>Compare competitors to identify unanswered questions, not to copy their positioning. Your own strengths and customer experience should determine the message.</p></section>
<section><h2>4. Select the features that support the purpose</h2><p>An online store needs clear product information, a usable checkout and secure payment handling. A lead-generation website needs a practical contact path and a way to respond. An educational resource needs clear navigation and helpful next steps. A support area needs answers people can find.</p><p>Add features because they help a customer complete the intended task. A chatbot, downloadable guide or long form is useful only when it improves that experience and someone can maintain it.</p><p>Choose a primary call to action that describes what happens next, such as “Request an estimate.” Give readers access to related information without making every link compete for attention.</p></section>
<section><h2>5. Map questions to pages</h2><p>A homepage introduces the business. A service page explains a specific offer. An about page builds understanding and trust. FAQs and articles answer questions that require more space.</p><p>Connect these pages with descriptive links. Visitors should be able to move from a question to a service explanation and then to a relevant contact option. Our <a href="/resources/website-guide-service-businesses/">website planning guide</a> covers that structure in more detail.</p></section>
<section><h2>A practical example</h2><p>Imagine a plumbing company that wants more scheduled water-heater consultations. This is a hypothetical example, not a client result.</p><p>The audience is homeowners within the company’s real service area who are comparing replacement options. A useful page would explain the service, common questions, relevant qualifications and what happens during a consultation. Its primary action could be requesting a consultation; a secondary action could be reading a replacement guide.</p><p>The page should not promise emergency availability, specific savings or installation times unless the business can support those claims.</p></section>
<section><h2>Use this planning worksheet</h2><div class="worksheet"><ol><li><strong>Audience:</strong> Who is this page for?</li><li><strong>Need:</strong> What problem or question brings them here?</li><li><strong>Offer:</strong> What can we actually provide?</li><li><strong>Coverage:</strong> Where is the service available?</li><li><strong>Proof:</strong> What credible evidence helps the visitor decide?</li><li><strong>Primary action:</strong> What should happen next?</li><li><strong>Secondary action:</strong> What helps someone who is not ready?</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> How will we know the intended action completed?</li></ol></div></section>
<section><h2>Measure completion, then improve</h2><p>Pageviews show activity. They do not prove that a business goal was achieved. Track completed purchases, qualified inquiries, confirmed bookings or another outcome appropriate to the page.</p><p>Check the full experience: a submitted form must reach the right person, a booking must be confirmed, and mobile visitors must be able to use the same essential features. Review the quality of the outcomes alongside the totals.</p><p>If your site attracts visitors but does not generate useful inquiries, read <a href="/resources/blog/contractor-website-not-converting/">why contractor websites fail to convert</a>. For help applying the plan, explore <a href="/services/web-design/">Holy Webs web design services</a>.</p></section></article></div>
